  "quality_flags": [
    {
      "flag_type": "unresolved_inconsistency",
      "slot": "exception_flow",
      "detail": "[exceptionflow_exit_coverage] Exception flow claim 0 only says the system requests that the logging-in user accept or deny command control. It does not document what happens after either decision, so the branch lacks a clear outcome such as returning to main_flow step 4, handing off control, rejecting the operation, or terminating. (still no supporting evidence after retrieval)",
      "evidence_refs": []
    },
    {
      "flag_type": "unresolved_inconsistency",
      "slot": "exception_flow",
      "detail": "[exceptionflow_exit_coverage] Exception flow claim 0 handles a command-control conflict by asking the logging-in user to accept or deny command control, but it does not state any outcome after accept or deny (e.g., gaining command control, rejection, return to main flow step 4, or termination), so the branch lacks a clear exit/recovery state. (still no supporting evidence after retrieval)",
      "evidence_refs": []
    },
    {
      "flag_type": "unresolved_inconsistency",
      "slot": "exception_flow",
      "detail": "[exceptionflow_exit_coverage] Exception flow claim 1 says the prior command-control user is notified when a new user takes over, but it does not specify whether the flow returns to main_flow, terminates/hands off, or ends in an explicit failure/prevention state after notification. (atomic grounding: candidate remains incomplete (Evidence that the takeover completes with command control handed off to the new user.))",
      "evidence_refs": []
    },
